John H. wrote:
> azgor wrote:

> > [4]: Sitting on gold should be comfortable, and exercise charisma, when
> > polymorphed into a dragon. - SpeedEvil
> Too special purpose, and charisma is one of the two stats (along with
> intelligence) that exist outside the exercise system.

I think the 'dragon sitting on a pile of gold' image is too
deeply-entrenched to be ignored by nethack, even if it only gets the
"comfortable" message rather than uncomfortable.

That said, I seem to remember somewhere (maybe it was The Hobbit?) that
by lying on a pile of treasure, a dragon presses all the gold into it's
underbelly, thus creating a makeshift suit of armour for its most
vulnerable part. Maybe nethack dragons could #sit on a big pile of gold
to gain an AC boost. Just one or two points. The pile of gold required
would have to be XP-dependent (we can pretend that XP=size of dragon)
and cost more than priest-protection. Any AC gained this way would of
course be lost when the player polymorphs out of dragon form. If you
wanted to be really authentic, you could require that the player #sits
on the armour and stays sat there for a certain number of turns, or
maybe even require that the dragon sleeps (which would be tricky for
the orange ones.)

Of course it would be really cool if NPC dragons understood and
exploited this feature "The silver dragon picks up 2000 gold pieces
--more--
the silver draon picks up 1600 gold pieces --more--
the silver dragon drops 3600 gold pieces --more--
the silver dragon sits on a hoarde of gold --more--
the silver dragon's underbelly gleams with a layer of gold!

Not sure how the game would handle the weight of this 'armour', or the
re-appearance of the gold when the dragon changes out of non-dragon
form, but I'm sure someone who knows the code could think of something.
Maybe the invention of a new armour item, "golden belly-armour" that is
created *only* when a dragon sits on a sufficient heap of cash, and
reverts back to a big pile of gold as soon as it is removed from the
dragon. (ie dragon dies or polymorphs, gold is somehow stolen from the
dragon)
 "Destroy armour" would vanish all the gold into nothingness, as you'd
expect.

This idea opens up a whole set of sub-YANIs, for example leprechauns
and the like stealing away bits of gold from the armour, gradually
degrading its effectiveness, and/or the odd gold piece falling off as
the dragon moves about, and the inclusion of gems in the treasure pile
for added AC... but I won't mention those.


